The desk merged every "wrong date" report into [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) because the subject
lines matched ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The subjects match. The
bugs don't. The desk's own cause table already shows three problems. The desk closed #4471 on
5 October with an answer for two of them. The third is still open.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table counts the way the desk files: by tenant and channel. The footnote under it is the
third problem. Those reports stay counted under `parser`, where they're filed, and get their own
ticket as well.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day moves are not fixed. Ticket #3 is still open.
</Callout>

## Where it stands

| problem | what the desk did on 5 October | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | fixed: "fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales" | [#1](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/1), closed |
| `hint` | won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired | [#2](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/2), closed as not planned |
| one-day moves | nothing | [#3](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/3), open |

Neither change reaches the one-day moves. They aren't a day/month swap, so reading the day first
doesn't help, and none of them came in through the booking-v3 form.

## `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant in #4471 writes dates day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so
8 June comes back as 6 August. This is the problem the incident page describes. The desk shipped
the fix on 5 October. It reached every way in except the booking-v3 form: the app, email, and the
booking-v2 form, whose hint already says `DD/MM/YYYY`.

Ticket [#1](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/1).

## `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says. The form then
reads the date the tenant's way, day first, and the booking lands with day and month swapped. The
symptom looks exactly like `parser`, but fixing the parser doesn't change it. The desk retired
the booking-v3 form instead of fixing the hint, so no new booking can hit this.

Only reports that came in through the form, at a tenant on booking-v3, belong here. The table
marks this row as having no ticket of its own. It has one now.

Ticket [#2](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/2).

## One-day moves: a date that moves by one day

These are the reports in the footnote. They describe no swap. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May.
Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same
meeting." The date shifts by a day, and it shifts differently for people in different places.
That reads like a time zone bug, a date stored as midnight in one zone and shown in another. Day
order has nothing to do with it, so the parser fix and the retired form leave these as they were.

The desk spotted them by reading (`desk-notes.json`). One reporter says they stopped reporting
these, so the footnote is a floor.

Ticket [#3](https://github.com/tunderadev/mega-support-kb/issues/3).

## Telling them apart

1. Read what moved. Day and month swapped means `parser` or `hint`. One day off is a one-day move.
2. For a swap, check how the report came in. Through the form at a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`.
   Anything else is `parser`.
3. Ignore the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, not the tenant, and the
   table shows how often it guesses wrong.
4. Don't merge on subject. The same subject lines turn up under different problems.

